 {'clause':'Ordinary one-gate difference with arbitrary ring scalars','assessment':'For a selected index k whose actual far triple is critical, injectivity shows that every j different from k samples a noncritical triple. All factors in the product over univ.erase k are consequently equal between H1 and H2. The proof separates the k factor from each complete finite product by mul_prod_erase, rewrites the common remaining product, and distributes the subtraction. The changed factor is -(H2(t)-H1(t))*inverse(2), so the result is negative delta times the complete product of remaining negative far factors. This uses arbitrary CommRing with invertible two; no field, ordering, nonzero scalar or geometric sign premise is added. Exactly-once occurrence describes the varying input gate, not a claim that the numerical product difference is nonzero for arbitrary ring arrays.'},
